export const debate = {"id":"bertuzzi-schieber-evil-evidence-2019","number":"116","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Cameron Bertuzzi vs Justin Schieber: The Problem of Evil","label":"Evil as evidence","date":"2026-08-26","duration":"1 hr 05 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=huOwbSrl-xw","motion":"Do facts about pain, pleasure, languishing, and horrendous evil count as evidence for naturalism over theism, or can soul-building and skeptical theism neutralize that evidential force?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Schieber's comparative suffering case prevailed overall, while Bertuzzi's likelihood objection and limited-information defense exposed a serious weakness in the final skeptical-theism reply.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"at best what follows is that naturalism predicts the data","context":"Bertuzzi distinguishes naturalism's likelihood assignment from the comparative ordering required to show confirmation over theism."},"con":{"text":"only that it predicts them more than does theism","context":"Schieber clarifies that comparative evidence requires a relative likelihood advantage, not absolute probability or entailment."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Theistic no-evidence defense","speaker":"Cameron Bertuzzi","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Draperian evil evidence","speaker":"Justin Schieber","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":78,"con":87},"sections":[{"sectionId":"comparative-likelihood-framework","title":"Comparative Likelihood and Naturalistic Prediction","timebox":"15:40–64:14","score":{"pro":86,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-comparative-naturalistic-prediction-c004","time":"15:40","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Schieber has not established naturalism's prediction because evolutionary background information either assists both hypotheses or improperly supplies explanatory resources unavailable to naturalism alone.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply targets the shared likelihood premise beneath all three opening arguments and usefully asks whether evolutionary background information independently supports naturalism rather than merely redescribing observations already placed into the evidential background. Principal limitation: Prediction, explanation, and background enrichment partly change functions within the objection, and the claim that additional information makes every hypothesis predict the data lacks a demonstrated bridge for each suffering class. Live burden: Bertuzzi must show that the permitted background collapses or equalizes the likelihood comparison, while Schieber must specify independently disciplined background commitments and distinguish relative prediction from a demand for complete explanation. Locked score: The strong band reflects broad decisive contact and an intelligible methodological challenge, moderated by conceptual instability, limited case-specific demonstration, and incomplete charity toward comparative confirmation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-comparative-prediction-standard","time":"27:51","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Naturalism need only make the suffering facts more expected than theism does; it need not entail them or make them probable in isolation.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely distinguishes absolute probability from comparative likelihood and correctly identifies the evidential standard needed for the cited facts to favor naturalism without requiring entailment or probability above one-half. Principal limitation: The correction does not entirely dispose of Bertuzzi's stronger concern, because a relative comparison still requires independently warranted likelihood assignments and disciplined background information rather than a complete naturalistic explanation. Live burden: Schieber must demonstrate the relative ordering for each principal data class, while Bertuzzi must avoid substituting an unnecessarily demanding explanatory standard for the actual comparative requirement. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional diagnostic contact, conceptual clarity, calibration, and central relevance, with only a bounded representational limitation arising from its incomplete treatment of the background-dependence objection.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-unknown-theistic-likelihood-candidate-001","time":"62:58","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Naturalism's prediction of suffering does not establish confirmation over theism when the likelihood of that same evidence under theism remains genuinely unknown.","score":93,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply cleanly distinguishes a known likelihood under one hypothesis from the comparative ordering required for confirmation and pinpoints the missing premise in the claim that naturalism outperforms an agnostic theistic assessment. Principal limitation: Its conclusion is conditional on genuine absence of informative bounds, so it would not answer an argument deriving an upper constraint from known divine goodness, power, knowledge, empathy, or independently defended moral expectations. Live burden: Bertuzzi must maintain that the targeted exchange supplies no warranted theistic bound, while Schieber must establish such a bound rather than treating uncertainty itself as inferiority. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding logical coherence, precision, calibration, responsiveness, charity, and central burden contact, with only the bounded vulnerability inherent in its expressly assumed unknown likelihood.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-background-does-not-entail-data","time":"29:07","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Common descent and transgenerational genetic change do not entail observed flourishing and languishing, leaving those facts available for a comparative likelihood assessment.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly separates general evolutionary commitments from the disputed observations and supplies a coherent compatibility test showing that common descent and genetic change could obtain without entailing the cited suffering facts. Principal limitation: Logical non-entailment alone does not establish probabilistic independence, evidential neutrality, or symmetrical application, so the answer leaves open whether the selected background already makes important features of the data substantially expected. Live burden: Schieber must justify the composition and evidential use of the background, while Bertuzzi must demonstrate probabilistic contamination rather than relying on the broader claim that information enrichment helps every hypothesis. Locked score: The very strong band reflects a precise, direct, well-calibrated answer to the entailment objection, tempered by subsidiary burden placement and an unresolved distinction between strict entailment and probabilistic dependence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"pain-pleasure-orientation","title":"Biological Orientation of Pain and Pleasure","timebox":"2:04–41:17","score":{"pro":78,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-biological-analogy-challenge-candidate-05","time":"38:20","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Alternative naturalistic assignments of biological and moral roles remain possible, so the analogy cannot confidently establish naturalism's expected organization of pain and pleasure.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly challenges the asserted naturalistic expectation by constructing an alternative assignment in which other systems serve biological functions while pain and pleasure primarily serve moral roles, thereby exposing underdetermination in the analogy. Principal limitation: Merely possible role assignments do not carry equal likelihood, and the move does not compare evolutionary mechanisms that might specifically favor affective systems closely tracking survival, injury, reproduction, and resource acquisition. Live burden: Bertuzzi must show mechanism-level parity or defective background selection, while Schieber must demonstrate that selection-supported expectations privilege the observed biological orientation over the proposed alternative. Locked score: The strong band reflects clear recoverability, cautious calibration, broad direct contact, and a relevant subsidiary challenge, limited by sparse probabilistic support and insufficient engagement with mechanism-based expectations.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-pain-biological-orientation","time":"2:04","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Pain and pleasure primarily serve biological goals and appear morally random, a pattern Schieber argues is more expected on naturalism than on morally perfect theism.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument identifies a concrete contrast between biologically directed sensations and the morally directed guidance plausibly anticipated from an omnipotent, perfectly good creator, thereby supplying a relevant and readily understandable comparative datum. Principal limitation: Its characterization of the system as morally random receives little independent demonstration, and the excerpt does not establish how frequently biological usefulness and moral usefulness diverge across representative cases. Live burden: The naturalistic side must substantiate the claimed distribution, while the theistic side must explain why predominantly biological orientation remains expectable given divine moral purposes and possible indirect contributions to agency. Locked score: The very strong band reflects central burden contact, coherent comparative framing, and explicit qualification, tempered by comparatively lighter empirical warrant and limited engagement with developed alternatives.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-pain-soul-building-mismatch","time":"32:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Soul-building predicts morally guiding sensations, yet observed pain and pleasure remain deeply biologically oriented and apparently random with respect to moral development.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply tests the proposed theodicy against the distinctive content of the evidence, arguing that soul-building should orient affect toward moral development whereas the actual system appears predominantly biological and morally irregular. Principal limitation: The argument only lightly documents moral randomness and compresses the possibility that biologically useful sensations indirectly sustain agency, stable embodiment, responsibility, and later opportunities for moral formation. Live burden: Schieber must establish why indirect developmental benefits do not predict the observed tuning, while Bertuzzi must explain why a morally purposive designer would choose this particular orientation rather than a comparably stable but more morally informative system. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent contact, precise internal testing, coherent comparative reasoning, and strong charity, with a limited evidential reservation concerning frequency and distribution.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"sentient-languishing","title":"Sentient Flourishing and Languishing","timebox":"5:25–23:37","score":{"pro":76,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-languishing-soul-building-c005","time":"18:18","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Significant suffering can enable loving virtues, belong within an overall good life, and ultimately be defeated, making suffering no less probable on theism.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move offers a substantive theistic mechanism rather than bare compatibility, linking significant suffering with costly virtues, an integrated good life, and eventual defeat while expressly presenting its likelihood conclusion as modest and nonexhaustive. Principal limitation: The extension from human moral agents to the full population of sentient beings remains unstable, equal likelihood is insufficiently established, and the distinctive biological orientation of pain and pleasure is not contacted. Live burden: Bertuzzi must explain nonhuman scope, observed proportions, necessity, and why less harmful developmental means would be inadequate, while Schieber must show those gaps materially lower the relevant theistic likelihood. Locked score: The strong band recognizes a coherent partial answer to languishing and horrendous evil, tempered by incomplete responsiveness, underdeveloped distributional warrant, and unresolved alternative-means objections.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-languishing-distribution","time":"5:25","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Competition, finite resources, and overpopulation systematically produce sentient languishing, whose prevalence and distribution fit indifferent natural processes better than purposive theism.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move connects widespread languishing to specified mechanisms, including finite resources, overpopulation, and indifferent evolutionary competition, rather than merely asserting that suffering exists, and it directly advances a central comparative data class. Principal limitation: The excerpt does not document the relevant proportions or demonstrate that the selected mechanisms characterize a representative range of sentient lives, so its language of entire expectation outruns the displayed empirical support. Live burden: Schieber must establish prevalence and comparative distribution more fully, while Bertuzzi must show why a perfectly good creator would permit those mechanisms to generate extensive nonhuman as well as human languishing. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes precise causal structure, substantial relevance, and clear inferential direction, while preserving the locked concern about underdeveloped proportional evidence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"horrendous-evil","title":"Horrendous Evil and Divine Prevention","timebox":"7:59–46:23","score":{"pro":71,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-horrendous-evil-defeat-candidate-06","time":"41:15","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Freedom, possible afterlife development, and eventual defeat mean horrendous evil need not permanently destroy a soul within a broader developmental account.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply usefully distinguishes severe present injury from permanent destruction and places freedom, continued development, eventual defeat, and saint-producing possibilities within a broader account of a person's complete life. Principal limitation: Those possibilities neither establish necessity nor support the compressed fine-tuning inference, and eventual recovery does not explain the observed incidence, original preventability, nonhuman scope, or absence of less harmful transformative arrangements. Live burden: Bertuzzi must connect afterlife development and freedom to comparative prediction rather than mere compatibility, while Schieber must show why even integrated recovery leaves the permission substantially surprising. Locked score: The mixed band reflects meaningful but partial contact with permanence and destructive effect, accompanied by clear modal relevance, while preserving serious deficits in warrant, precision, alternative-means responsiveness, and proportional explanation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-horrendous-evil-surprise","time":"7:59","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Profound suffering, disfigurement, and devastating loss are less surprising under morally indifferent naturalism than under perfectly informed, resourceful, and compassionate theism.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument appropriately frames horrendous evil through comparative surprise rather than claiming logical incompatibility, and it identifies harmful behavioral tendencies as an intelligible naturalistic route to severe interpersonal devastation. Principal limitation: The excerpt supplies a broad category and an explanatory sketch but comparatively little detail about incidence, preventability, background conditions, or the precise expectations generated by perfect divine compassion. Live burden: Schieber must defend the likelihood contrast across representative horrendous evils, whereas Bertuzzi must explain why morally significant freedom, later recovery, or other goods make their actual severity and distribution reasonably expected. Locked score: The strong band reflects clear relevance, coherent calibration, and bounded scope, while recognizing that its empirical warrant and treatment of developed theistic alternatives remain less complete than its central importance.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-horrendous-evil-soul-destruction","time":"35:11","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Horrendous evil can cause severe moral injury and destroy rather than build souls, despite divine access to less destructive routes toward moral development.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument directly reverses the theodicy's beneficial-effect premise by identifying severe moral injury, loss of life's perceived worth, and soul-destroying outcomes within the very category offered as material for development. Principal limitation: The excerpt establishes that horrendous evil can be destructive but does not fully demonstrate its characteristic long-term effects, the frequency of eventual integration, or every tradeoff associated with divine prevention. Live burden: Schieber must connect destructive incidence and preventability to the comparative likelihood, while Bertuzzi must show why possible recovery, freedom, or later transformation makes permitting the original devastation necessary rather than merely reparable. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise scope, powerful direct contact, calibrated modal language, and charitable recognition of recovery, with modest empirical and counterfactual development remaining.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"soul-building-defense","title":"Scope and Necessity of Soul-Building","timebox":"33:32–64:37","score":{"pro":70,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-joint-soul-building-conclusion-candidate-002","time":"64:11","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Bertuzzi closes by combining soul-building with continued skeptical-theist agnosticism as a joint defense against Schieber's probabilistic argument from evil.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: The closing synthesis connects the two principal defensive routes and correctly emphasizes that a successful global likelihood neutralizer could matter even if particular soul-building details remain contestable. Principal limitation: It categorically declares success without answering the later objections concerning nonhuman scope, destructive outcomes, proportional distribution, compensation, necessity, and less harmful alternatives, while leaving the combination's inferential basis largely unstated. Live burden: Bertuzzi must explain how the routes jointly overcome those outstanding objections without double counting, whereas Schieber must show that skeptical uncertainty does not independently neutralize the otherwise stronger theodicy critique. Locked score: The mixed band reflects direct motion-level relevance and some continuing skeptical-theist contact, but also material overstatement, missing qualification, weak new warrant, and a significant nonanswer to decisive soul-building challenges.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-soul-building-scope-alternatives","time":"33:32","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human soul-building leaves most sentient languishing unexplained, while an unlimited God could use less harmful transformative experiences such as vivid dreams or visions.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies two independent and central deficiencies in the theodicy: its restricted application to human moral agents and the apparent availability of less harmful transformative means to an unlimited and empathic creator. Principal limitation: Calling dreams or visions categorically better compresses possible differences in embodied agency, responsibility, interpersonal stakes, epistemic distance, and the developmental consequences of confronting actual rather than represented hardship. Live burden: Schieber must show that plausible divine aims remain achievable through materially less destructive arrangements, while Bertuzzi must explain both vast nonhuman suffering and why the observed severity is necessary for goods unavailable through alternatives. Locked score: The very strong band reflects direct, charitable, and highly relevant engagement with scope and necessity, tempered by comparatively limited defense of the proposed substitutes.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-afterlife-compensation-insufficient","time":"50:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Animal afterlives do not predict suffering's proportions, while later compensation neither justifies preventable harm nor excludes less destructive means of transformation.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply carefully separates prediction, compensation, justification, necessity, and alternative means, showing that an animal afterlife or eventual benefit does not by itself explain the observed proportions or permission of preventable harm. Principal limitation: The excerpt says comparatively little about the strongest integration account, on which suffering becomes an intelligible part of an individual's complete good rather than receiving merely external or later compensation. Live burden: Schieber must show that integration still fails proportionality and necessity, while Bertuzzi must explain nonhuman distribution and why dreams, visions, or other less harmful experiences cannot secure the relevant developmental goods. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, coherent distinctions, calibrated insufficiency, precise scope, and charitable engagement, with only limited development of the strongest complete-life alternative.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"skeptical-theism","title":"Skeptical Theism and Unknown Reasons","timebox":"23:34–56:38","score":{"pro":85,"con":77},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-epistemic-chasm-c006","time":"23:34","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Human cognitive distance from divinity leaves the probability of suffering on theism unknown, so naturalistic prediction alone cannot establish comparative confirmation.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply isolates the exact comparative requirement shared by the opening arguments and explains that knowing one hypothesis's likelihood does not establish an ordering when the competing theistic likelihood remains epistemically unavailable. Principal limitation: Its categorical no-evidence consequence depends on defending total unavailability, because known divine attributes might still supply directional constraints or defensible bounds even when complete divine reasons remain inaccessible. Live burden: Bertuzzi must justify why human limitations defeat every useful likelihood estimate rather than merely counseling caution, while Schieber must derive an ordering without treating uncertainty as a determinate middling value. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent responsiveness, central relevance, and explicit probabilistic focus, moderated by analogy-heavy warrant and an insufficient answer to the possibility of informative lower-order bounds.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unknown-reasons-tie","time":"36:49","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Unknown reasons may oppose divine permission as readily as support it, so they confer no favorable presumption and leave known comparative reasons operative.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply blocks an asymmetrical appeal to mystery by observing that unknown moral reasons could favor prevention as readily as permission, thereby refusing to let unspecified possibilities automatically outweigh articulated comparative evidence. Principal limitation: Symmetry among unknown reasons does not itself establish that known reasons are representative, probabilistically dominant, or sufficient for estimating the total theistic likelihood, and skeptical theism need not posit a favorable hidden pool. Live burden: Schieber must justify why known considerations retain directional force despite acknowledged sampling limits, while Bertuzzi must explain why those limits defeat useful bounds rather than merely weakening confidence. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct contact, explicit structure, and central relevance, moderated by a material representational shortfall and an incomplete bridge from symmetry to evidential priority.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-agnosticism-limited-sample-candidate-07","time":"46:21","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Skeptical theism invokes insufficient, potentially unrepresentative information rather than favorable unknown reasons, requiring agnosticism about suffering's total probability on theism.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply accurately distinguishes suspension of judgment from an appeal to favorable hidden reasons and reframes skeptical theism as a sampling challenge concerning radically incomplete and potentially unrepresentative moral information. Principal limitation: Limited sampling warrants caution but does not automatically establish categorical agnosticism, because known divine attributes or robust moral constraints might still yield informative bounds without representing the entire unknown reason set. Live burden: Bertuzzi must justify radical unrepresentativeness and the absence of useful bounds, while Schieber must show that known directional considerations support an ordering despite incomplete access to the total reasons. Locked score: The very strong band reflects sharp diagnostic correction, explicit qualification, substantial charity, and central relevance, moderated by an underdeveloped bridge from incompleteness to wholesale likelihood suspension.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-agnostic-theism-relative-likelihood","time":"53:40","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Even granting agnosticism about theism's prediction, Schieber argues that naturalism can outperform it because naturalism assigns the suffering data a definite likelihood.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly accepts skeptical-theist agnosticism for argument's sake and squarely addresses its claimed consequence, maintaining focus on whether naturalism can still obtain comparative evidential support. Principal limitation: A known naturalistic likelihood cannot be shown greater merely because the theistic likelihood is unknown; uncertainty is not a determinate middling likelihood, so the asserted ordering lacks the indispensable comparative premise. Live burden: Schieber must supply an upper bound or other warranted constraint on the theistic likelihood, while Bertuzzi must defend why no known attributes provide such a constraint. Locked score: The mixed band reflects excellent relevance and responsiveness to the decisive issue, but also a materially overstated conclusion, partly unstable comparison, weak inferential coherence, and insufficiently charitable treatment of genuine agnosticism.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":78,"strengths":["Precisely identified that a known naturalistic likelihood does not by itself establish comparative confirmation when the theistic likelihood remains genuinely unknown.","Corrected the portrayal of skeptical theism as reliance on favorable hidden reasons by presenting it as a limited and potentially unrepresentative sampling problem.","Directly challenged the background information and analogical basis used to generate naturalistic expectations rather than conceding those likelihood assignments.","Offered soul-building, freedom, complete-life integration, and eventual defeat as substantive theistic mechanisms instead of resting on logical compatibility alone."],"blunders":[{"text":"The closing synthesis declared the soul-building routes successful without answering the strongest objections about nonhuman scope, destructive effects, proportionality, necessity, and less harmful alternatives.","links":[]},{"text":"Several theodicy claims moved from possible recovery or development to comparative probability without supplying the required distributional and counterfactual support.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":87,"strengths":["Clearly defended the correct comparative-likelihood standard and distinguished relative prediction from entailment or high absolute probability.","Developed three mutually reinforcing data classes involving biological orientation, widespread languishing, and horrendous evil.","Pressed the soul-building account on its weakest dimensions: nonhuman scope, destructive outcomes, observed proportions, preventability, and less harmful alternatives.","Separated afterlife compensation from prediction, justification, necessity, and complete explanation of the observed suffering pattern."],"blunders":[{"text":"The final skeptical-theism reply treated an unknown theistic likelihood too much like a determinate inferior likelihood, leaving the claimed comparative ordering unsupported.","links":[]},{"text":"The unknown-reasons symmetry argument answered an asymmetric appeal to hidden reasons more effectively than the actual limited-sample case for suspended judgment.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest theistic defense is a bounded underdetermination claim: the cited suffering facts cannot favor naturalism unless their comparative likelihood advantage survives disciplined background selection, developed theistic purposes, and justified limits on human access to the relevant reasons.","premises":["Comparative confirmation requires warranted likelihoods or defensible bounds for both hypotheses; a likelihood assigned only under naturalism cannot establish an ordering against a genuinely unconstrained theistic likelihood.","Evolutionary background information may legitimately inform naturalistic prediction, but its selection must remain independent of the disputed suffering observations and must be applied symmetrically when compatible with theism.","A developed soul-building account must address humans and nonhuman animals separately, distinguish developmental hardship from destructive excess, and explain why actual harms provide goods unavailable through materially gentler alternatives.","Skeptical theism should claim graded evidential restraint rather than automatic neutrality: incomplete access weakens confidence only to the extent that known divine attributes fail to impose robust directional constraints."],"conclusion":"Accordingly, the suffering data provide no established comparative advantage for naturalism unless the naturalistic likelihoods are independently supported and the proposed theistic bounds, purposes, and epistemic limitations are shown inadequate."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Mechanism-matched comparison","text":"A fair likelihood comparison should match explanations at the same level of detail. If naturalism is enriched with evolutionary selection, ecological scarcity, developmental psychology, and social competition, theism should likewise be evaluated with a specified account of stable agency, regular natural laws, creaturely independence, and complete-life goods. This does not make either account true. It prevents a thin theism from being compared with a mechanism-rich naturalism and clarifies which additional commitments genuinely predict the observations."},{"title":"Heterogeneous suffering partition","text":"The evidential force of suffering should be partitioned rather than treated uniformly. Ordinary developmental frustration, biologically useful pain, nonhuman predation, accidental catastrophe, and intentionally inflicted horrendous evil may have different likelihood relations under each hypothesis. A theistic defense could concede that some classes favor naturalism while arguing that others are neutralized by agency, regularity, or complete-life goods. The resulting case would be more modest, but it would avoid requiring one theodicy to explain every phenomenon in the same way."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The strongest naturalistic case is cumulative and bounded: several independently specified features of suffering are less surprising under morally indifferent processes than under perfectly informed, powerful, and compassionate purposive governance.","premises":["Biologically oriented affect, ecological scarcity, nonhuman languishing, and severe moral injury arise through identifiable natural mechanisms that are indifferent to whether their outcomes advance moral development.","Perfect divine empathy, knowledge, and resourcefulness create at least defeasible expectations of reduced destructive excess, broader concern for nonhuman welfare, and use of less harmful means when equivalent goods remain available.","A theodicy predicts the observations only when it explains their scope, proportions, severity, and necessity; showing possible benefit, recovery, or later compensation establishes compatibility but not comparative expectation.","Skeptical uncertainty does not authorize a numerical comparison by itself, but known divine attributes can support upper bounds when the observed harms appear gratuitously destructive relative to available alternatives."],"conclusion":"Therefore, naturalism receives cumulative comparative support only insofar as these independently defended mechanisms and attribute-based bounds make the observed pattern more expected than the developed theistic alternatives do."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Cross-class convergence","text":"The three evidence classes gain force if their likelihood advantages arise from different mechanisms. Biological affect tracks survival pressures, ecological languishing follows scarcity and reproductive competition, and horrendous interpersonal evil follows fallible agency operating without guaranteed protection. A separate theistic auxiliary story may accommodate each class, but repeatedly adding distinct purposes, hidden constraints, and future remedies increases theoretical dependence on unsupported auxiliaries. The argument is not that complexity proves naturalism; it is that convergent predictions can be evidentially stronger than disconnected accommodations."},{"title":"Preventive substitution test","text":"For any alleged soul-building good, compare the actual harm with the least destructive arrangement plausibly capable of producing a similar good. The relevant question is not whether suffering can ever contribute to courage, compassion, or maturity, but whether its intensity, victims, duration, and collateral effects are needed for that contribution. When severe harms predictably destroy agency or affect beings unable to develop the cited virtues, the substitution test supplies an attribute-based reason to expect prevention under perfect theism without claiming complete access to divine reasons."}]}}};
